ge": "**Yes, one real problem: `somelib==1.1` is GPL-3.0.**\n\n| Dependency | License | Closed-source risk |\n|---|---|---|\n| requests 2.32 | Apache-2.0 | Fine. Keep the license and NOTICE attribution. |\n| left-pad 0.2 | MIT | Fine. Keep the copyright and license text. |\n| **somelib 1.1** | **GPL-3.0** | **Blocker if you distribute it.** |\n\n**Why GPL-3.0 is a problem**\n- If you ship a binary, installer, app or container to customers and it includes or links `somelib`, the combined work generally has to be released under GPL-3.0 with source available. That conflicts with a closed-source product.\n- GPL-3.0 also has anti-tivoization and patent-licensing terms, which matter if you ship hardware or locked-down devices.\n- If you only run it on your own servers as a SaaS and never distribute it, the GPL's copyleft isn't triggered. That changes if `somelib` is actually AGPL, so check the real license text. Your lawyers should confirm this.\n\n**Options**\n1. Replace `somelib` with a permissively licensed alternative (MIT, BSD or Apache).\n2. Ask the `somelib` authors for a commercial or dual license.\n3. Isolate it in a separate process that you don't distribute. This is legally subtle, so get counsel's sign-off.\n4. Open-source your product under a GPL-compatible license, if that's acceptable.\n\n**Other checks**\n- `deps.txt` lists only direct dependencies. `requests` pulls in `urllib3`, `idna`, `certifi` and `charset-normalizer`, and `somelib` may have its own dependencies. Scan the full transitive tree, for example with `pip-licenses` or `pip show`.\n- The license strings in `deps.txt` are self-declared. Verify them against each package's actual metadata and LICENSE file, because a package's license can differ from what's listed.\n- Add a third-party notices file to the release for the Apache and MIT attributions.\n- `left-pad` is on its own a trivial package. Confirm it's the one you mean, since typosquatted look-alikes exist.\n\nI only read `deps.txt`. I haven